283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 138 0 R
/T (1.1 Background and Motivation Object tracking is a fundamental problem in computer vision with wide-ranging applications in surveillance, traffic monitoring, robotics, sports analytics, and autonomous systems. In real-world environments, identifying and continuously tracking moving objects from video streams enables systems to understand motion patterns, detect anomalies, and support intelligent decision-making. Traditional monitoring systems rely heavily on manual observation, where human operators visually track objects across video feeds. This process is inefficient, error-prone, and not scalable for large-scale deployments such as city-wide surveillance or traffic management systems. With the increasing availability of cameras and video data, there is a growing need for automated systems that can perform object tracking accurately and in real time. Computer vision techniques provide a powerful solution to this challenge. By leveraging image processing algorithms, it is possible to track objects without requiring manual intervention. This project focuses on implementing a real-time object tracking system using the CSRT \(Discriminative Correlation Filter with Channel and Spatial Reliability\) algorithm, which offers improved accuracy and robustness compared to basic tracking methods. The system is designed to operate efficiently on standard CPU hardware without the need for deep learning models or high-end GPUs, making it suitable for low-cost and real-time applications.)
/E (1.1 Background and Motivation Object tracking is a fundamental problem in computer vision with wide-ranging applications in surveillance, traffic monitoring, robotics, sports analytics, and autonomous systems. In real-world environments, identifying and continuously tracking moving objects from video streams enables systems to understand motion patterns, detect anomalies, and support intelligent decision-making. Traditional monitoring systems rely heavily on manual observation, where human operators visually track objects across video feeds. This process is inefficient, error-prone, and not scalable for large-scale deployments such as city-wide surveillance or traffic management systems. With the increasing availability of cameras and video data, there is a growing need for automated systems that can perform object tracking accurately and in real time. Computer vision techniques provide a powerful solution to this challenge. By leveraging image processing algorithms, it is possible to track objects without requiring manual intervention. This project focuses on implementing a real-time object tracking system using the CSRT \(Discriminative Correlation Filter with Channel and Spatial Reliability\) algorithm, which offers improved accuracy and robustness compared to basic tracking methods. The system is designed to operate efficiently on standard CPU hardware without the need for deep learning models or high-end GPUs, making it suitable for low-cost and real-time applications.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
