426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 147 0 R
/T (The bounding box defines the initial region of interest \(ROI\) containing the target object to be tracked. It is specified by its top-left coordinates along with width and height, typically selected manually from the first video frame. Accurate bounding box selection is essential, as it determines the initial features learned by the CSRT tracker. A tightly fitted box ensures that the tracker focuses on the object while minimizing background interference. If the bounding box is too large, irrelevant background features may degrade tracking performance, whereas a very small box may fail to capture sufficient object detail. Proper initialization improves tracking accuracy, stability, and trajectory consistency across frames.)
/E (The bounding box defines the initial region of interest \(ROI\) containing the target object to be tracked. It is specified by its top-left coordinates along with width and height, typically selected manually from the first video frame. Accurate bounding box selection is essential, as it determines the initial features learned by the CSRT tracker. A tightly fitted box ensures that the tracker focuses on the object while minimizing background interference. If the bounding box is too large, irrelevant background features may degrade tracking performance, whereas a very small box may fail to capture sufficient object detail. Proper initialization improves tracking accuracy, stability, and trajectory consistency across frames.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 147 0 R
/T (Why CSRT: The CSRT algorithm employs correlation filters with channel and spatial reliability weighting to improve tracking robustness. It evaluates multiple feature channels \(such as color and texture\) and assigns reliability scores to focus on the most informative regions of the object. Unlike simpler trackers such as MOSSE or KCF, which may drift under occlusion or scale changes, CSRT adapts dynamically to variations in object appearance. This results in more stable tracking, accurate bounding box updates, and reliable trajectory generation across frames.)
/E (Why CSRT: The CSRT algorithm employs correlation filters with channel and spatial reliability weighting to improve tracking robustness. It evaluates multiple feature channels \(such as color and texture\) and assigns reliability scores to focus on the most informative regions of the object. Unlike simpler trackers such as MOSSE or KCF, which may drift under occlusion or scale changes, CSRT adapts dynamically to variations in object appearance. This results in more stable tracking, accurate bounding box updates, and reliable trajectory generation across frames.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
