1126 0 obj
<<
/Title (The proposed pipeline begins with a preprocessing stage that applies multiple image enhancement techniques, including frame resizing, Gaussian denoising, grayscale conversion, and histogram equalization. These operations improve visual clarity and tracking stability under varying lighting conditions, motion blur, and noise commonly found in real-world video streams. The core tracking module initializes a bounding box around a selected object in the video frame. The CSRT tracker then continuously updates the object position across successive frames by learning correlation filters and spatial reliability maps. Unlike detection-based systems, this approach does not require a pre-trained dataset and operates efficiently on standard CPU hardware.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1114 0 R
/Prev 1120 0 R
/Next 1136 0 R
>>
endobj
1127 0 obj
<<
/Title (For evaluation, the system measures tracking performance using frame-wise success rate and accuracy metrics, indicating how consistently the object is tracked throughout the video sequence. Experimental results demonstrate reliable tracking performance in traffic video scenarios, with stable bounding box alignment and smooth trajectory generation.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1114 0 R
/Prev 1136 0 R
/Next 1121 0 R
>>
endobj
1128 0 obj
<<
/Title (1.1 Background and Motivation Object tracking is a fundamental problem in computer vision with wide-ranging applications in surveillance, traffic monitoring, robotics, sports analytics, and autonomous systems. In real-world environments, identifying and continuously tracking moving objects from video streams enables systems to understand motion patterns, detect anomalies, and support intelligent decision-making. Traditional monitoring systems rely heavily on manual observation, where human operators visually track objects across video feeds. This process is inefficient, error-prone, and not scalable for large-scale deployments such as city-wide surveillance or traffic management systems. With the increasing availability of cameras and video data, there is a growing need for automated systems that can perform object tracking accurately and in real time. Computer vision techniques provide a powerful solution to this challenge. By leveraging image processing algorithms, it is possible to track objects without requiring manual intervention. This project focuses on implementing a real-time object tracking system using the CSRT \(Discriminative Correlation Filter with Channel and Spatial Reliability\) algorithm, which offers improved accuracy and robustness compared to basic tracking methods. The system is designed to operate efficiently on standard CPU hardware without the need for deep learning models or high-end GPUs, making it suitable for low-cost and real-time applications.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1122 0 R
/Next 1137 0 R
>>
endobj
1129 0 obj
<<
/Title (To initialize and update bounding boxes dynamically to accurately follow the object across frames. To compute and store the centroid positions of the object for each frame. To generate a trajectory visualization representing the path of the moving object over time. To evaluate system performance using metrics such as tracking accuracy and frame-wise success rate. To develop a modular implementation using Python and OpenCV that can run efficiently on CPU without requiring deep learning frameworks.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1122 0 R
/Prev 1138 0 R
>>
endobj
