1145 0 obj
<<
/Title (2.1 Classical Object Tracking Methods Early research in moving object detection and tracking relied heavily on classical computer vision techniques that operate directly on pixel intensity and motion information. Background subtraction methods, such as the Gaussian Mixture Model \(GMM\) proposed by Stauffer and Grimson \(1999\), were widely used to detect moving objects by modeling the scene and identifying deviations from the background. While effective in controlled environments, these approaches were sensitive to illumination changes, noise, and dynamic backgrounds. Feature-based tracking methods were introduced to improve robustness. The Kanade-Lucas-Tomasi \(KLT\) tracker uses optical flow to track feature points across frames, providing efficient performance for small motions but struggling with large displacements and occlusions. Similarly, Mean Shift and CamShift algorithms track objects based on color histograms, offering simplicity and real-time performance but lacking robustness to scale variations and complex backgrounds. A significant advancement in classical tracking came with correlation filter-based methods. The MOSSE tracker introduced by Bolme et al. \(2010\) demonstrated high-speed tracking using adaptive correlation filters. Building on this, the Kernelized Correlation Filter \(KCF\) improved accuracy by incorporating multi-channel features, though it remained limited in handling scale and spatial variations. To overcome these limitations, Lukezic et al. \(2017\) proposed the CSRT tracker, which incorporates channel and spatial reliability into the correlation filter framework. This improves tracking accuracy under challenging conditions such as occlusion and deformation. The CSRT algorithm forms the foundation of this project, enabling reliable real-time object tracking and trajectory analysis without requiring deep learning models or extensive training data.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1140 0 R
/Next 1152 0 R
>>
endobj
1146 0 obj
<<
/Title (2.3 Gap Identified Most existing research in object tracking focuses either on deep learning-based detection frameworks or on standalone classical tracking algorithms, often treating them as separate approaches. Deep learning methods require large annotated datasets and GPU resources, while classical methods are sometimes limited to simple scenarios without comprehensive motion analysis. Additionally, many systems focus only on object detection rather than continuous tracking and trajectory generation. This project addresses the gap by implementing a lightweight CSRT-based tracking system that operates in real time on CPU, generates trajectory information, and requires no training data.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1140 0 R
/Prev 1152 0 R
>>
endobj
